### Compile JavaScript to Hermes Bytecode

Source: https://context7.com/discord/hermes/llms.txt

Use `hermes::compileJS` to compile JavaScript source code into Hermes bytecode from C++. Supports custom diagnostic handlers, source maps, and optimization flags. A simplified overload is available for basic compilation.

```cpp
#include <hermes/CompileJS.h>
#include <iostream>

// Custom diagnostic handler to collect errors and warnings
class MyDiagHandler : public hermes::DiagnosticHandler {
 public:
  void handle(const Diagnostic &diag) override {
    const char *kind = diag.kind == Kind::Error ? "Error"
                     : diag.kind == Kind::Warning ? "Warning" : "Note";
    std::cerr << kind << " at line " << diag.line
              << ", col " << diag.column
              << ": " << diag.message << "\n";
  }
};

int main() {
  std::string jsSource = R"(
    'use strict';
    function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
    print(add(1, 2));
  )";

  std::string bytecode;
  MyDiagHandler diagHandler;

  // Full-featured compilation
  bool ok = hermes::compileJS(
      jsSource,
      "myScript.js",   // sourceURL shown in stack traces
      bytecode,
      /*optimize=*/true,
      /*emitAsyncBreakCheck=*/false,
      &diagHandler
  );

  if (ok) {
    std::cout << "Compiled successfully. Bytecode size: "
              << bytecode.size() << " bytes\n";
    // bytecode can now be written to a .hbc file or passed to HermesRuntime
  } else {
    std::cerr << "Compilation failed.\n";
    return 1;
  }

  // Simplified overload (optimize=true by default)
  std::string bc2;
  hermes::compileJS("var x = 42;", bc2);

  return 0;
}

```

